  "text": "### Step 1: Understanding the Problem\n\nWe need to find all positive integers \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) divides the product \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). In other words, \\( n + 2 \\) must be a divisor of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 2: Setting Up the Divisibility Condition\n\nLet’s denote \\( d = n + 2 \\). Since \\( d \\) is a positive integer, \\( d \\) must divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\nGiven \\( d = n + 2 \\), we can express \\( n \\) as \\( n = d - 2 \\). Substituting this into the expression \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n\n\\[\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(d - 2 + 3)(d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)((d - 2)^2 + 9)\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( d \\) must divide \\( 3(d + 1)((d - 2)^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 3: Simplifying the Expression\n\nLet’s simplify \\( (d - 2)^2 + 9 \\):\n\n\\[\n(d - 2)^2 + 9 = d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9 = d^2 - 4d + 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the divisibility condition becomes:\n\n\\[\nd \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n\\]\n\nSince \\( d \\) divides \\( 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\), it must also divide \\( 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\) modulo \\( d \\).\n\n### Step 4: Analyzing the Modulo Condition\n\nConsider the expression modulo \\( d \\):\n\n\\[\n3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\equiv 3(1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\pmod{d}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\n3(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\equiv 3 \\cdot 13 \\equiv 39 \\pmod{d}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( d \\) must divide \\( 39 \\).\n\n### Step 5: Finding Possible Values of \\( d \\)\n\nSince \\( d \\) divides \\( 39 \\), the possible values of \\( d \\) are the positive divisors of \\( 39 \\). The positive divisors of \\( 39 \\) are \\( 1, 3, 13, 39 \\).\n\n### Step 6: Finding Corresponding Values of \\( n \\)\n\nRecall that \\( d = n + 2 \\). Therefore, for each divisor \\( d \\) of \\( 39 \\), we can find \\( n \\):\n\n1. If \\( d = 1 \\), then \\( n = d - 2 = -1 \\). But \\( n \\) must be positive, so discard this case.\n2. If \\( d = 3 \\), then \\( n = 1 \\).\n3. If \\( d = 13 \\), then \\( n = 11 \\).\n4. If \\( d = 39 \\), then \\( n = 37 \\).\n\n### Step 7: Verifying the Solutions\n\nWe need to verify that for each \\( n \\), \\( n + 2 \\) divides \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n1. For \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   n + 2 = 3 \\\\\n   3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(4)(10) = 120 \\\\\n   3 \\mid 120 \\quad \\text{(True)}\n   \\]\n2. For \\( n = 11 \\):\n   \\[\n   n + 2 = 13 \\\\\n   3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3(14)(130) = 5460 \\\\\n   13 \\mid 5460 \\quad \\text{(True)}\n   \\]\n3. For \\( n = 37 \\):\n   \\[\n   n + 2 = 39 \\\\\n   3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3(40)(1378) = 164880 \\\\\n   39 \\mid 164880 \\quad \\text{(True)}\n   \\]\n\nAll three values of \\( n \\) satisfy the condition.\n\n### Step 8: Calculating the Sum of Valid \\( n \\)\n\nThe valid positive integers \\( n \\) are \\( 1, 11, 37 \\). Their sum is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 11 + 37 = 49\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{49}\n\\]",
